<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ullusunu"><span class="enc-name">Ullusunu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ullusunu was a king of Mannaea in north-western Iran in the late 8th century BCE, son of Iranzu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulpian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulpian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulpian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulpian was a Roman jurist of the early third century CE and one of the most influential legal writers in Roman history.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulric-manfred-ii"><span class="enc-name">Ulric Manfred II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulric Manfred II was margrave of Turin from about 1000 until his death in 1034, of the Arduinici family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-east-frisia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-i-count-of-east-frisia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich I, Count of East Frisia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich Cirksena was a Frisian chieftain who, after a century of feuds among the chiefs of East Frisia, brought most of the region under his rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-i-count-of-wurttemberg"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich I, Count of Württemberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich I, Count of Württemberg, known as the Founder, ruled Württemberg from about 1241 to 1265.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg-stargard"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich II, Duke of Mecklenburg-Stargard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich II (d. 1471) was the last Duke of Mecklenburg-Stargard, a branch of the Mecklenburg dynasty that had ruled the south-eastern Lordship of Stargard since 1352.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-rosch"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-rosch.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich Rösch</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich Rösch was abbot of Saint Gall from 1463 until his death in 1491, after serving as its administrator from 1457.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-von-jungingen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich von Jungingen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich von Jungingen was Grand Master of the Teutonic Order from 1407 to 1410.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-wille"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-wille.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich Wille</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich Wille was a Swiss army officer who served as commander-in-chief, with the rank of general, during the First World War, from 1914 to 1918.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-mecklenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-duke-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, was administrator of the Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin from 1550 and ruled Mecklenburg-Güstrow from 1555 until his death in 1603.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulrich, Duke of Württemberg, ruled from 1498 to 1550.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulster-covenant"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulster-covenant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulster Covenant</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ulster Covenant was a pledge signed by nearly half a million Unionist men and women on and after 28 September 1912, Ulster Day, to resist the Third Home Rule Bill…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulster-cycle"><span class="enc-name">Ulster Cycle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ulster Cycle is a body of medieval Irish heroic tales about the Ulaid, their king Conchobar mac Nessa and the warriors of his court at Emain Macha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulster-unionist-party"><span class="enc-name">Ulster Unionist Party</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ulster Unionist Party is a unionist political party in Northern Ireland, founded in 1905 as the Ulster Unionist Council to oppose Irish home rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulster-volunteers"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulster-volunteers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulster Volunteers</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ulster Volunteers were a Unionist militia founded in 1912 and organised in January 1913 as the Ulster Volunteer Force to resist Home Rule for Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulugh-beg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulugh Beg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulugh Beg was a Timurid ruler and astronomer, a grandson of Timur, who governed Samarkand for decades and died in 1449.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulugh-beg-observatory.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulugh Beg Observatory</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ulugh Beg Observatory was an astronomical observatory built in Samarkand in the 1420s by the Timurid ruler Ulugh Beg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-khan"><span class="enc-name">Ulugh Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulugh Khan was a general of the Delhi Sultanate and a brother of Sultan Alauddin Khalji.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ulugh-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ulugh Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ulugh Muhammad was a Chinggisid khan of the Golden Horde who, after losing power in the struggles of the early 15th century, moved to the middle Volga and around 1438…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/uluj-ali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uluj Ali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uluj Ali was an Italian-born Ottoman corsair and admiral, born in Calabria as Giovanni Dionigi Galeni and captured by Barbary corsair