 of Umarkot, but refused his wealth and stayed…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/umar-sanda-kyarimi"><span class="enc-name">Umar Sanda Kyarimi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Umar Sanda Kyarimi was Shehu of Borno from 1937 to 1968, ruling from Maiduguri under British colonial rule and into Nigerian independence.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/umar-walasma"><span class="enc-name">Umar Walasma</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Umar Walasma was the founder of the Walashma dynasty and of the Sultanate of Ifat in the eastern highlands of Shewa, Ethiopia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-umari"><span class="enc-name">al-Umari</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Umari was a fourteenth-century Arab historian and administrator in Mamluk Egypt and Syria, author of an encyclopaedic work on geography and administration.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/umaru-dallaji"><span class="enc-name">Umaru Dallaji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Umaru Dallaji was a Fulani scholar and follower of Usman dan Fodio who led the jihad in Katsina, capturing the city from its Hausa ruler in 1807.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/umaru-nagwamatse"><span class="enc-name">Umaru Nagwamatse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Umaru Nagwamatse was the founder of the Kontagora Emirate in western Nigeria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/umayyad-caliphate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Umayyad Caliphate</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Umayyad Caliphate was the second Islamic caliphate, ruled by the Umayyad family from Damascus from 6